# demo-arith — fire fixture for .formwork/rules/todo-fixme-hack-shell.yaml.
#
# Three shapes that made the destring-sh lexer read a `<<` as a heredoc opener,
# queue a body, and blank every remaining line looking for a terminator that
# does not exist. The failure is SILENT: a blanked comment produces no finding,
# so the gate reports OK on the whole rest of the file.
#
# All three markers must fire. They cannot be split across files: each door
# blinds everything after it, so a single file is what proves the later ones
# are still reachable.
set -euo pipefail

n=8

# `(( ... ))` is bash's arithmetic COMMAND. Only the `$(( ... ))` EXPANSION was
# tracked as an arithmetic span, so the `<<` here read as an opener named `n`.
(( mask = 1 << n ))
# after an arithmetic command, then TODO: bound the shift    # want: todo-fixme-hack-shell

# Every argument of `let` is an arithmetic expression, and none of it was
# tracked at all.
let other=1<<n
# after a let expression, then FIXME: bound the shift        # want: todo-fixme-hack-shell

printf '%s %s\n' "${mask}" "${other}"

# The general net under both: a heredoc whose terminator never arrives is not a
# heredoc. Blanking it to EOF is the blindness; reading it as shell can only
# ever produce a LOUD false positive, so that is the fail-closed direction.
# Keep this last — everything below it is the unterminated body.
cat <<NOPE
a body line whose terminator never arrives
# inside an unterminated heredoc, then XXX: bound it         # want: todo-fixme-hack-shell
